+## 11. Reader identities and names
+
+Current convention, 30 October 2026. The source owner is
+[`recensions.json`](../src/sources/calendars/recensions.json), schema
+`triptych-roman-recensions/v2`, validated by `scripts/_recensions.py` and
+`scripts/_missal_versions.py`. It keeps three identities separate:
+
+1. **Book state or reform instrument:** the identified edition or act, with
+   its own authority and date. A decree's promulgation or effective date is
+   distinct from a book's publication year.
+2. **Manifestation:** the actual language, publisher, printing or reprint,
+   edition statement and exact source artifact. The Bonn 2004 reprint of
+   edition XXVIII conformed to the Vatican typical edition remains distinct
+   from the original Vatican 1920 impression. Its reprinted decree establishes
+   no identity with that impression or an exact 1948 printing. Its inferred,
+   internally layered content horizon of about 1954 remains separate from its
+   firm publication year.
+3. **Reader view:** the material Triptych actually serves through a supported
+   reading route. It can combine sources whose wording, calendar model and
+   relationship to the named book differ. Each unit keeps those qualifications.
+
+### Registered keys and display names
+
+New book keys use the form `roman-<language>[-<territory>]-<publication-year>-<edition-or-witness>`;
+reader keys name the supported view. An edition reader can retain a declared
+edition key such as `roman-1962`; a mixed projection names its witness or book
+base and ends in `-study`. These are registration conventions, never a parser
+that manufactures an option from a plausible name. The catalog's `books` and
+`reader_views` own the exact keys and source relationships.
+
+The current public reader keys are `roman-bonn-2004-study`, `roman-1962` and
+`roman-2008-study`. The declared default remains the 1962 view. Display names
+come from `reader_version.label` and `short_label`; the formal book citation
+comes from the linked book's `citation`, projected as `book_label`. A source
+title, storage directory or reform boundary cannot supply a replacement label.
+The catalog retains scholarly states and expressions that have no reader view.
+In particular, the abstract 1920 book state has no authenticated calendar
+association with the Bonn study projection.
+
+The Bonn view retains [section 10.4](#104-three-exact-bonn-witness-bodies-separately-from-inherited-residence)'s
+three separately collated Pentecost Latin bodies. Other inherited Proper and
+English material remains sourced to the resident 1962 corpus, and the Ordinary
+to its identified 1861 lay witness. Their availability establishes no Bonn,
+Vatican-1920 or 1948 attestation. The 2008 study view combines its book base
+with later source-recorded calendar additions; neither a single frozen cutoff
+nor a complete-current calendar horizon is established. The base book year,
+applied variation acts and dataset revision remain separate facts. Many wording
+comparisons use a secondary 2002 reproduction or the 1981 Lectionary. No
+complete authenticated 2008 altar book is held and no served body is graded
+exact to that target. The catalog owns those current correspondence limits.
+
+### Readable contribution and correspondence
+
+A selectable reader view must contribute a nonempty body in at least one
+language through the existing publication filters and a supported reading
+route. English is not required. Actual served Bible or source text may
+contribute through its declared route, with its reconstruction or source
+qualification intact. A title, index, incipit, citation, expected slot, empty
+translation, absence or restricted remote metadata alone does not qualify.
+Scholarly catalog visibility remains independent of reader admission.
+
+`scripts/_missal_versions.py` derives `reader_contribution.eligible` from the
+owning producers' published bodies, separately from their coverage totals.
+Removing the last served body removes the reader option. Availability,
+publication basis and target correspondence remain separate assertions: a
+qualified historical or study body can contribute without acquiring an
+`exact-target` grade. A reader name changes none of the body's language,
+witness, rights, collation, appointment or absence evidence.
+
+### Compatibility and presentation
+
+The declared legacy inputs `roman-pre-1955` and `postconciliar` resolve to the
+same Bonn and 2008 study objects respectively. Public shares encode the
+registered reader key. These are lexical aliases, not source-book equivalences:
+an abstract 1920 state is not Bonn, and a secondary 2002 reproduction is not
+2008. Unknown explicit reader keys, duplicate or ambiguous alias declarations,
+and repeated semantic URL keys refuse explicitly.
+
+Decode only declared reader selections in URLs, preferences and CLI inputs;
+normalize before calendar-directory filtering or closed dispatch. Internal
+calendar, source, form, appointment, event and source-qualified choice IDs stay
+unchanged. Unknown compatibility-envelope fields remain opaque. The
+[reader-state contract](liturgy-reader-state.md#legacy-url-compatibility)
+owns the shared decoder/encoder API and the unchanged v1 semantic boundary.
+
+Identity, unavailable responses, Study, Compare and print retain the descriptor's
+formal book citation and full correspondence limits. Naming or admission alone
+does not complete a book, establish a calendar cutoff, grant publication rights
+or close a recorded wording gap. Historical prose, literal source titles,
+source IDs, body hashes and sealed review records retain their original bytes.
